data.content:
## What breaks: Sync I/O on the Cosmos DB provider is **fully removed** in EF Core 11

In .NET 10, you could opt in to sync-over-async with the Cosmos provider. In EF Core 11, **that opt-in no longer exists** — all synchronous I/O calls **always throw**, regardless of configuration.

Every method in your `ProductRepository` is affected:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();       // was ToList()
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id); // was First()
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();        // was Count()
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();                  // was SaveChanges()
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- All methods become `async Task<T>` (or `async Task`)
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`, `First()` → `FirstAsync()`, `Count()` → `CountAsync()`, `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`
- All callers up the chain must also be made async

This will ripple through your codebase — any interface (`IProductRepository`) and all callers need matching async signatures.
